WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:04.940
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.320
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:09.180
We were on The Office together, and we're best friends.

4
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:13.320
And now we're doing the ultimate Office Lovers podcast just for you.

5
00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:17.800
Each week, we will dive deeper into the world of The Office with exclusive interviews,

6
00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.000
behind-the-scenes details, and lots of BFF stories.

7
00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.040
We're the Office Lady 6.0.

8
00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:26.120
Hello.

9
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:27.080
Hi.

10
00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.320
How are you, lady?

11
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:29.200
I'm good.

12
00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:30.080
Me too.

13
00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.740
I need an update from us.

14
00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:33.420
Oh, yeah?

15
00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:35.860
I want to check in on our New Year's resolution.

16
00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.240
To get off the hamster wheel, how's it going?

17
00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.220
Well, pretty good.

18
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.260
I hamstered a little bit on Saturday.

19
00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:43.120
I know you did.

20
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:44.440
And then I put it away.

21
00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:45.660
I put work away.

22
00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.960
And then I have started purging my closet.

23
00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:52.580
This is what I did with this extra time.

24
00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:53.140
Yes.

25
00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.160
And I'm so happy about it.

26
00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:58.740
And also, like, I went through, and there's some stuff in my closet that I'm like,

27
00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.760
it doesn't really fit me anymore, but I still like the stuff.

28
00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:03.280
And I put together, like, a little pile.

29
00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.300
And Isabel got to go through it all, and she was so excited.

30
00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:08.100
It was like shopping in mom's closet.

31
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:09.220
Oh, I'm sure.

32
00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:09.660
Yeah.

33
00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:11.640
Because you guys are the same size.

34
00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:12.280
AlmoSt.

35
00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.000
But her feet are bigger than yours, right?

36
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.180
Her feet are—she's tinier than I am, but, like, overall, but her feet are bigger,

37
00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.600
which makes her crazy because she's like, oh, you have so many great shoes.

38
00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:27.200
Well, I have added on to getting off the hamster wheel.

39
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:33.220
I have added on doing one thing at a time, which I found has been really good for me.

40
00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:39.860
I think I used to pride myself on being an expert multitasker, and I want to become

41
00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:41.820
an expert single tasker.

42
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.300
I like that.

43
00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:44.260
Single tasking.

44
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:44.560
You know what?

45
00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:45.980
Multitasking's overrated.

46
00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:47.340
I think so.

47
00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:52.840
And I think, again, especially as a person who works from home, I don't want to put in

48
00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:57.080
a load of laundry while also answering an email.

49
00:01:57.320 --> 00:01:58.980
So I've stopped doing that.

50
00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:03.880
I have work hours, and I have home hours, and I'm actually finding that I'm happier,

51
00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:11.420
more present, and more efficient in those spaces when I divide them up rather than try to, like,

52
00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:13.360
make them work together.

53
00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:14.720
I like this.

54
00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:16.640
I've started doing this thing.

55
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.340
Tell me if you think it's passive-aggressive.

56
00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:20.840
I'm excited.

57
00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:22.100
I'm sitting up.

58
00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:22.500
Okay.

59
00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:28.800
So whenever someone's on a device in my house, like, we start a conversation,

60
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:32.400
and then maybe they check an email or look at a text.

61
00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:34.000
I stop talking.

62
00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:34.900
Oh.

63
00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:36.440
Just mid-sentence.

64
00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:37.020
I love this.

65
00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:37.700
I stop talking.

66
00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:42.080
And then, inevitably, they realize, and they look up, and I go,

67
00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:42.580
Hey, there.

68
00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:43.900
Let me know when you're ready.

69
00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:46.320
Oh, lady.

70
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:47.740
I'm living for this.

71
00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:48.400
Here's the thing.

72
00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:50.100
I'm trying to have a conversation.

73
00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:50.420
Yeah.

74
00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:53.980
I don't want to, like, have a conversation with the top of your head while you're looking

75
00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:54.560
at a device.

76
00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:56.280
And I have a house full of teenagers.

77
00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:59.800
We're learning our boundaries with devices, right?

78
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:00.280
Mm-hmm.

79
00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:02.220
Social cues and whatnot.

80
00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:02.660
Yes.

81
00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:06.700
And I think this is a good life skill to take out with you that when someone's talking to

82
00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:08.140
you, you focus on them.

83
00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:12.700
And then, if you have an email or something you need to check, then you go do it.

84
00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:13.860
I like this.

85
00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:15.560
I think this is smart.

86
00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:16.960
I think this is good parenting.

87
00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:23.260
Maybe it's passive-aggressive if you want to give it that label, but it's also proactive

88
00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:25.300
life lessening.

89
00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:25.880
Okay.

90
00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:27.240
How about that spin on it?

91
00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:29.640
There's our self-help book.

92
00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.700
Proactive life lessening by Jenna and Angela.

93
00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:34.460
All right, everyone.

94
00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.880
Welcome to this week's Office Lady 6.0.

95
00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:44.280
Today, we are going to be breaking down the third episode of Season 1's The Paper, titled

96
00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:45.460
The Buddy and the Dude.

97
00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:46.200
Yeah.

98
00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:51.700
But before we dive into that, we are going to kick things off with a chit-chat sent in from

99
00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:52.680
Steve T.

100
00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:54.480
from Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania.

101
00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:56.280
Hi, ladies.

102
00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:58.360
This is Steve from Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania.

103
00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:03.060
I'm curious what your preferred thermostat setting is, both in the summer and winter.

104
00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:08.200
My fiancé and I both like to keep the house pretty cold, so any warmer than 68 degrees

105
00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:09.380
and we feel like we're roasting.

106
00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:12.620
That is not the question I was expecting.

107
00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:14.280
But okay.

108
00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:15.780
What were you expecting?

109
00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:16.920
I don't know.

110
00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:20.480
I wasn't expecting a thermostat question, but I sort of love it.

111
00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:22.360
It's very timely for me, Steve.

112
00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.080
I don't know how you knew this.

113
00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:31.420
Well, I loved this question because this has been a theme in my life, the thermostat war.

114
00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:32.080
Yeah.

115
00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:34.540
I mean, especially on the set of The Office.

116
00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:41.300
We had Steve Carell, who ran hot, and so on the days he was there, the air was like

117
00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:46.520
cranked down, and then we were all freezing and we would have little heaters under our desk.

118
00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:49.860
But I guess I should also talk about what it's like at home.

119
00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:52.560
Well, he's asking our preferred setting.

120
00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:53.020
Yeah.

121
00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:54.800
What is yours, Lainey?

122
00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:02.280
Well, my preferred setting is 70 degrees air conditioning at night.

123
00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:06.100
Really, all I care about right now, Steve, is what is the temperature at night when I'm

124
00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:07.520
trying to sleep through my hot flashes.

125
00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:07.960
Sure.

126
00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:11.780
But right now in LA, it's getting very cold at night.

127
00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:12.320
Mm-hmm.

128
00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:18.060
And so the problem is when I'm going to sleep, I need the air conditioner on, but then the

129
00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:23.400
house might get all the way down to like 66 degrees overnight if the heat isn't on.

130
00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:23.920
Yeah.

131
00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:25.940
I'm really in a pickle.

132
00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:27.500
I'm fighting with it.

133
00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:28.980
I'll get up in the middle of the night.

134
00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:32.460
I'm worried the kids are cold, so then I turn the heat on and then I'm sweating.

135
00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:37.240
This is a long story to say my preferred setting is 70 degrees.

136
00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:37.900
At night.

137
00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:39.060
At any time.

138
00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:39.480
Okay.

139
00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:46.220
70 seems to be my sweet spot, but I live in a very old house and different rooms run hot

140
00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:47.800
and other rooms run cold.

141
00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:48.030
Yes.

142
00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:49.880
And it's very hard to keep everybody happy.

143
00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:52.260
So we have a few space heaters.

144
00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:56.360
My son sleeps with his window open a little bit because his room runs hot.

145
00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:57.700
It's a whole thing.

146
00:05:58.100 --> 00:05:59.480
I'm going to throw you a curveball.

147
00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:00.340
What is it?

148
00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:02.760
Your face is so funny.

149
00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:03.820
Why?

150
00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:05.320
You're like, what is it?

151
00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:07.720
I turn off the heat at night.

152
00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:08.360
I know.

153
00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:08.840
Me too.

154
00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:09.580
That's what I'm saying.

155
00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:10.160
No, no.

156
00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:11.640
But like I let it get down.

157
00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:13.400
Like it doesn't kick on ever.

158
00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:16.860
And I sleep with the window open and it gets cold.

159
00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:20.840
Like you wake up and the house is cold, but I like to sleep in a cold room.

160
00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:21.680
I'm with you, Steve.

161
00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:24.680
I'm like, I tell the kids, guys, pile on the blankets.

162
00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:25.260
Okay.

163
00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:28.000
And I sleep with the window open no matter where I am.

164
00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:31.300
So like we go to see Josh's family in Colorado in the winter.

165
00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:32.460
I open that window.

166
00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:33.740
You know, that's really healthy.

167
00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:34.840
I was reading about it.

168
00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:35.200
Oh, yeah?

169
00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:38.540
We're supposed to be getting more fresh air into our homes than we're getting.

170
00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:39.060
Oh.

171
00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:43.320
Like you're supposed to, remember in ye olden times when there was no like,

172
00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:45.220
you know, HVAC systems?

173
00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:45.620
Yeah.

174
00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:47.380
You had your windows open a lot.

175
00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:47.720
Yeah.

176
00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:50.680
And that's really good to get that fresh air.

177
00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:51.520
I'm that person.

178
00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:53.120
I have windows open all over the house.

179
00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:53.560
Smart.

180
00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:56.320
I like it around 64 degrees at night.

181
00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:57.000
64?

182
00:06:57.460 --> 00:06:57.900
Yes.

183
00:06:58.280 --> 00:06:58.700
Yes.

184
00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:01.660
That's what the thermostat said this morning when I woke up.

185
00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:03.700
It said it was 64 degrees in the house.

186
00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:04.120
Mm-hmm.

187
00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:09.160
And I just, oh, man, I just layer these heavy blankets and I'm so snug.

188
00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:14.360
But it is hard for me to get out of bed in the morning because I'm so snug.

189
00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:15.640
There you go.

190
00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:16.840
70 and 64.

191
00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:17.680
Yeah.

192
00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:18.880
Those are our numbers.

193
00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:19.760
Thanks, Steve.

194
00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:20.740
Loved your question.

195
00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:22.760
Well, listen, why don't we take a break?

196
00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:26.740
Because when we come back, we've really got a lot of fun stuff to share about this episode.

197
00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:27.340
Yes.

198
00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:29.080
We reached out to Greg Daniels.

199
00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:29.480
Yes.

200
00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:34.680
And I traded some texts with writer Patrick Kang and also Chelsea Frye.

201
00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:37.500
And we have a little something-something from Creed Bratton.

202
00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:39.360
I'm really excited about that one.

203
00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:39.700
I know.

204
00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:40.200
Me too.

205
00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:52.920
Hey, we are back.

206
00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:55.660
Let's discuss today's The Paper episode.

207
00:07:55.660 --> 00:08:00.140
It is season one, episode three, titled The Buddy and the Dude.

208
00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:03.880
It is written by Patrick Kang and Michael Levin.

209
00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:07.200
Guess what show Patrick worked on before?

210
00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:09.360
Like, back in the day.

211
00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:10.660
You're not going to guess.

212
00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:11.480
I'm going to tell you.

213
00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:12.800
How I met your mother.

214
00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:14.280
I might have guessed.

215
00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:16.400
I didn't give you time to guess.

216
00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:20.320
I was thinking, like, oh, it's got to be something we're connected to.

217
00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:20.600
Yes.

218
00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:21.540
That's where my brain was going.

219
00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:21.920
Okay.

220
00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:22.900
Isn't that crazy?

221
00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:23.380
Yeah.

222
00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:24.060
Small world.

223
00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:24.540
I know.

224
00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:27.820
And this episode was directed by Yana Gorskaya.

225
00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:29.960
Are you ready for a summary?

226
00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:30.720
Yes.

227
00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:31.600
All right.

228
00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:32.140
Here it is.

229
00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:38.520
In this episode, Ned scrambles to teach his staff about the importance of fact-checking

230
00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:39.060
sources.

231
00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:45.020
Mare pitches an undercover investigation into a shady mattress pricing scam.

232
00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:51.940
Meanwhile, Esmeralda continues her attempts to sabotage Ned, including revealing the fact

233
00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:55.940
that Mare might have a plan to leave the paper for a job in hospitality.

234
00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:57.860
All right.

235
00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:03.000
My single fast fact is a fun guest star crossover.

236
00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:08.300
Mary Hollis in Bowdoin plays Cindy, the mattress store employee.

237
00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:12.060
She's got a really great big guest star on this episode.

238
00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:18.280
You all might know her from her many roles on shows like HBO's The Righteous Gemstones or

239
00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:26.040
AMC's Kevin Can F himself, or maybe ABC's The Real O'Neils, which is where Angela, the two

240
00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:26.600
of you, met.

241
00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:26.980
Yep.

242
00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:31.660
You had a two-episode guest arc on that show, and I remember you telling me how much fun

243
00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:34.360
you had and how nice Mary was to you on the set.

244
00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:37.060
She's great, and I just enjoy her.

245
00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:40.460
Every time I'm around her, we've reconnected a few times throughout the years.

246
00:09:40.460 --> 00:09:41.500
She's just wonderful.

247
00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:44.260
And I reached out to her for this episode.

248
00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:45.020
You did.

249
00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:47.520
You asked her about her time on the paper.

250
00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:50.400
She sent us some great behind-the-scenes details.

251
00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:53.780
We're going to sprinkle those in as we break down this episode.

252
00:09:53.780 --> 00:09:58.700
But the first question you asked her, of course, was, how did you get your job on the paper?

253
00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:03.560
And it turns out she first auditioned for a series regular role on the show when they

254
00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:04.820
were casting the pilot.

255
00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:05.340
I know.

256
00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:06.180
How about that?

257
00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:14.200
She said she spent, quote, most of February 2024 auditioning under a cloak of secrecy.

258
00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:18.060
A month of auditioning for a series regular role on the show.

259
00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:19.720
That she knew nothing about.

260
00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:21.820
She said it, quote, could not be named.

261
00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:23.440
How crazy is that?

262
00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:26.060
All she knew was that Greg had created a show.

