00:21:19.640 --> 00:21:23.020
And later on, I had to sell it for some reason.

517
00:21:23.340 --> 00:21:29.920
And, I mean, I mean, God, this thing's probably worth $800,000 to a million dollars today, something like that.

518
00:21:30.800 --> 00:21:32.600
But it was a loss.

519
00:21:33.980 --> 00:21:34.540
Okay.

520
00:21:34.980 --> 00:21:35.480
Okay.

521
00:21:35.960 --> 00:21:39.380
I mean, I'm already just, I'm in.

522
00:21:39.640 --> 00:21:40.500
I'm waiting.

523
00:21:40.740 --> 00:21:41.080
I know.

524
00:21:41.240 --> 00:21:41.700
Here we go.

525
00:21:41.820 --> 00:21:42.300
Number two.

526
00:21:43.240 --> 00:21:52.820
In 1965, I was doing a movie in Israel, and I came on the set and was leaning against this wall.

527
00:21:53.620 --> 00:21:58.040
And unbeknownst to me, there were live squibs, explosives in the wall.

528
00:21:58.400 --> 00:22:03.380
And this gentleman walks over to me, and he says, hey, kid, you're going to blow your arm off or kill yourself.

529
00:22:03.540 --> 00:22:05.600
And he's kind of led me away from the thing.

530
00:22:06.120 --> 00:22:08.020
And that man was Frank Sinatra.

531
00:22:08.380 --> 00:22:11.160
He probably saved me losing my arm or my life.

532
00:22:11.720 --> 00:22:13.520
Thank you, Frank.

533
00:22:15.040 --> 00:22:15.600
Ooh.

534
00:22:15.960 --> 00:22:24.280
Now, that's a tricky one, because he told us in his All About Creed episode that he did that movie and that Frank Sinatra was in it.

535
00:22:24.540 --> 00:22:24.720
Yeah.

536
00:22:24.860 --> 00:22:28.500
So is he using a little bit of the truth?

537
00:22:28.660 --> 00:22:28.920
Mm-hmm.

538
00:22:28.980 --> 00:22:30.580
That's a very good strategy.

539
00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:31.180
I know.

540
00:22:31.600 --> 00:22:32.600
Oh, my gosh.

541
00:22:32.600 --> 00:22:34.480
This one's titled Plane Crash.

542
00:22:35.840 --> 00:22:49.120
It was either 67 or 1968, and I'm flying in a little Piper Cub over the Florida Panhandle and with the grassroots.

543
00:22:49.120 --> 00:22:53.800
And we're getting ready to land for this show.

544
00:22:54.280 --> 00:22:58.760
And we hear the guy saying over the radio, said, all right, the landing area there, blah, blah.

545
00:22:58.980 --> 00:23:01.240
So I got a red light and a green light.

546
00:23:02.460 --> 00:23:05.940
And I look down, and I see a blue, blue and a yellow light.

547
00:23:06.340 --> 00:23:12.380
So I started explaining to the guy, said, hey, you know, those aren't the colored lights.

548
00:23:12.620 --> 00:23:16.920
And he turns around and he goes, look, you play guitar, and I'll fly the airplane.

549
00:23:17.260 --> 00:23:18.980
So then, and I felt really bad.

550
00:23:18.980 --> 00:23:22.660
I had a bad feeling about this, you know, this landing.

551
00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:28.580
So we land on the runway, and there's debris all over, and it blows out the tire on the right.

552
00:23:28.720 --> 00:23:32.880
And we end up in a canal with one wing down into the water.

553
00:23:33.540 --> 00:23:35.520
Everyone's, you know, I'm calm.

554
00:23:35.640 --> 00:23:36.460
I'm cool because I'm Creed.

555
00:23:36.840 --> 00:23:38.120
I'm not, I'm chill.

556
00:23:38.680 --> 00:23:50.340
And next thing I know, over the hill, I see this light from a, you know, truck or something backlining this military guys with machine guns and dogs, these big, big Alsatians.

557
00:23:51.140 --> 00:23:53.700
And they got the guns on us, get out, get out of the planes.

558
00:23:53.920 --> 00:24:00.240
We start to crawl off the wing into the water, and I looked out in the water, and there's alligators.

559
00:24:00.460 --> 00:24:01.740
There's alligators in the water.

560
00:24:01.940 --> 00:24:08.720
And so now we're going to get attacked by the alligators or eaten by the dogs or shot by the soldiers.

561
00:24:09.160 --> 00:24:15.860
So they grabbed me and put handcuffs on me and took me off to the brig as I was on a restricted military base.

562
00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:19.240
Yeah, that's the story.

563
00:24:20.460 --> 00:24:21.740
So, holy crap.

564
00:24:21.980 --> 00:24:25.400
So we have to pick two of these are the truth, and one is the lie.

565
00:24:25.540 --> 00:24:25.760
Yeah.

566
00:24:26.840 --> 00:24:31.100
Okay, so we have guitar from drug deal.

567
00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:31.660
Right.

568
00:24:32.340 --> 00:24:34.480
Frank Sinatra saving his life.

569
00:24:34.660 --> 00:24:35.000
Mm-hmm.

570
00:24:35.900 --> 00:24:36.940
Or landing.

571
00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:38.900
Plane crash.

572
00:24:39.080 --> 00:24:42.880
Plane crash landing in an Air Force base?

573
00:24:43.120 --> 00:24:45.600
Yeah, a restricted Air Force base.

574
00:24:45.680 --> 00:24:46.280
Air area.

575
00:24:46.280 --> 00:24:46.540
Yeah.

576
00:24:47.080 --> 00:24:50.280
I think the lie is plane crash.

577
00:24:52.020 --> 00:24:54.020
I—but I think it's only half of a lie.

578
00:24:54.200 --> 00:24:55.340
This is my instinct.

579
00:24:55.720 --> 00:25:01.020
I think this thing with the lights did happen, and then I think the rest of it is a lie.

580
00:25:01.800 --> 00:25:02.320
Okay.

581
00:25:02.320 --> 00:25:10.540
I don't think there were alligators in the water, and I don't think that military people with guns took him away in handcuffs.

582
00:25:10.720 --> 00:25:12.600
Also, he named the type of dogs.

583
00:25:12.600 --> 00:25:14.080
That was so specific.

584
00:25:14.560 --> 00:25:14.920
Mm.

585
00:25:15.340 --> 00:25:17.800
I think the plane crash is the—oh, God.

586
00:25:17.920 --> 00:25:22.280
I don't know, because the guitar—like, he could know all that information.

587
00:25:22.440 --> 00:25:23.360
He has a ton of guitars.

588
00:25:23.360 --> 00:25:26.380
If you go to his house, he's got a wall of guitars.

589
00:25:26.500 --> 00:25:26.660
Yeah.

590
00:25:28.300 --> 00:25:32.960
So he very well could have owned that guitar, but maybe it didn't come from a drug deal.

591
00:25:33.860 --> 00:25:34.340
All right.

592
00:25:34.360 --> 00:25:35.020
Do you have the answer?

593
00:25:35.240 --> 00:25:36.300
Wait, are we going to guess?

594
00:25:36.300 --> 00:25:37.060
What's the lie?

595
00:25:37.180 --> 00:25:37.760
What do you say?

596
00:25:37.820 --> 00:25:38.220
I said plane crash.

597
00:25:38.240 --> 00:25:39.340
You say plane crash.

598
00:25:39.880 --> 00:25:40.240
Ugh.

599
00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:42.580
I'm going to say guitar.

600
00:25:43.000 --> 00:25:43.280
Okay.

601
00:25:43.680 --> 00:25:44.000
All right.

602
00:25:44.200 --> 00:25:45.840
We each picked a different one.

603
00:25:46.040 --> 00:25:46.240
Okay.

604
00:25:46.460 --> 00:25:47.260
Here we go.

605
00:25:47.480 --> 00:25:50.220
He titled the last audio clip, I Lied.

606
00:25:51.820 --> 00:25:52.380
Okay.

607
00:25:52.880 --> 00:26:01.580
The false one, the one I'm lying about is—wait for it—the 58 Gibson Korean Explorer.

608
00:26:01.780 --> 00:26:02.020
Nope.

609
00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:04.180
I never owned one of those.

610
00:26:04.180 --> 00:26:13.820
I owned a Les Paul, a 56 Black Beauty, a 335 Red Gibson, beautiful Gibson with a floating tailpiece.

611
00:26:14.140 --> 00:26:23.160
I now have a 69 Blues Bird and a 57 Country and Western, and a Blues King, a very nice little Parler Gibson guitar.

612
00:26:23.440 --> 00:26:26.280
But no 58 Explorer, unfortunately.

613
00:26:26.280 --> 00:26:27.280
So there we go.

614
00:26:27.440 --> 00:26:28.420
I Lied.

615
00:26:31.180 --> 00:26:34.800
Do you all realize now what it's like to hang out with Creed?

616
00:26:35.040 --> 00:26:38.820
How long has he been sitting on this plane crash story?

617
00:26:38.980 --> 00:26:39.380
I know.

618
00:26:39.540 --> 00:26:41.140
We've never heard this story.

619
00:26:41.620 --> 00:26:43.020
I mean, that's wild.

620
00:26:43.020 --> 00:26:48.020
So when he tells you a wild thing, you see why it might be true.

621
00:26:48.300 --> 00:26:49.400
Yeah, exactly.

622
00:26:50.020 --> 00:26:50.980
Yeah, I don't know.

623
00:26:51.100 --> 00:26:56.540
I just was like—there was a few details in that plane story that I thought were so specific.

624
00:26:56.540 --> 00:26:59.300
That's why I thought it was half-truth, half-lie.

625
00:27:00.060 --> 00:27:00.500
Wow.

626
00:27:00.680 --> 00:27:00.860
Wow.

627
00:27:01.060 --> 00:27:04.080
Creed, you got me, but you didn't get Angela.

628
00:27:04.640 --> 00:27:05.440
Thanks, buddy.

629
00:27:06.120 --> 00:27:07.700
Well, now we're in Ned's office.

630
00:27:07.700 --> 00:27:11.840
He's having a meeting with Esmeralda and Ken, and he tells them,

631
00:27:11.960 --> 00:27:14.360
guys, we're doing pretty good, but we're one article short.

632
00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:18.860
Esmeralda pretends to be concerned, and she has a talking head.

633
00:27:19.120 --> 00:27:20.780
She's like, Ned is still here.

634
00:27:21.180 --> 00:27:22.940
He must have cockroach DNA.

635
00:27:23.180 --> 00:27:25.440
You know, it's like you smash it, but it doesn't die.

636
00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:30.300
And she does an impression of like a cockroach on its back with its legs wiggling.

637
00:27:30.500 --> 00:27:31.440
She does this thing with her hands.

638
00:27:31.480 --> 00:27:32.600
It's very funny.

639
00:27:32.780 --> 00:27:35.480
It's very funny, and she also has really long fingernails.

640
00:27:35.700 --> 00:27:37.620
Like, she's very expressive with her hands.

641
00:27:37.840 --> 00:27:45.660
Well, writer Patrick Kang said that when she did this bit where she pretends to be the cockroach,

642
00:27:45.880 --> 00:27:47.420
everybody went wild.

643
00:27:47.580 --> 00:27:48.620
Like, it was so funny.

644
00:27:48.620 --> 00:27:54.760
And he said that they discovered that she is particularly funny whenever she is talking about

645
00:27:54.760 --> 00:27:57.660
or impersonating animals or creatures.

646
00:27:58.260 --> 00:28:01.840
He said this was a big bonus, and you're going to see this more throughout the season

647
00:28:01.840 --> 00:28:03.240
because they started writing for it.

648
00:28:03.560 --> 00:28:05.160
That is so funny.

649
00:28:05.860 --> 00:28:09.760
Well, Ken is going to suggest that they need their own version of Wordle to keep,

650
00:28:09.760 --> 00:28:12.240
you know, people's minds off the actual news.

651
00:28:12.800 --> 00:28:14.700
And Mare has an idea.

652
00:28:15.240 --> 00:28:18.100
Esmeralda is so startled when Mare speaks up.

653
00:28:18.260 --> 00:28:21.120
Yeah, Mare is like sitting behind Esmeralda.

654
00:28:21.320 --> 00:28:21.600
Yeah.

655
00:28:21.780 --> 00:28:25.820
And this is what I'm going to start calling Esmeralda Queen of Shade.

656
00:28:27.340 --> 00:28:31.740
And shade alert number one, Esmeralda turns to Mare and says,

657
00:28:31.840 --> 00:28:33.920
Ah, I thought you were a pile of shirts.

658
00:28:34.220 --> 00:28:35.460
A pile of shirts.

659
00:28:35.560 --> 00:28:37.500
What a great line.

660
00:28:37.820 --> 00:28:38.720
Such good writing.

661
00:28:38.860 --> 00:28:40.140
That's such a good dig.

662
00:28:40.960 --> 00:28:46.260
Mare proposes that they resurrect this column from the 90s called Shame on You,

663
00:28:46.460 --> 00:28:49.220
where they uncover a local business scam.

664
00:28:49.580 --> 00:28:52.180
She says she went shopping for a mattress recently,

665
00:28:52.180 --> 00:28:56.580
and she thinks that there's a false advertising scam at local mattress stores.

666
00:28:56.800 --> 00:28:57.780
Ned gets so excited.

667
00:28:58.000 --> 00:29:01.720
Well, the meeting's going to end, and Esmeralda is stewing.

668
00:29:02.120 --> 00:29:03.360
Yeah, she stays behind.

669
00:29:03.620 --> 00:29:06.740
Yeah, she's so annoyed that Ned likes Mare's ideas.

670
00:29:06.740 --> 00:29:11.160
So she decides to be sabotage-y, and she's like, oh, you know what?

671
00:29:11.500 --> 00:29:14.020
Mare's leaving for, you know, a hotel business.

672
00:29:14.260 --> 00:29:14.460
Yeah.

673
00:29:14.660 --> 00:29:17.800
She got accepted to this concierge training program at the Radisson.

674
00:29:18.300 --> 00:29:18.560
Mm-hmm.

675
00:29:18.900 --> 00:29:20.340
Ned is, like, bummed out.

676
00:29:20.500 --> 00:29:22.140
He's like, but she's my best reporter.

677
00:29:22.460 --> 00:29:23.820
And he starts to panic.

678
00:29:24.260 --> 00:29:27.060
We have an Esmeralda shade alert number two.

679
00:29:27.200 --> 00:29:27.620
We do.

680
00:29:27.980 --> 00:29:31.680
She says Mare, the woman whose parents named her after a horse.

681
00:29:31.680 --> 00:29:32.300
Yeah.

682
00:29:32.920 --> 00:29:34.380
Did you see her outfit, by the way?

683
00:29:34.620 --> 00:29:39.540
I thought it was symbolic a little bit, you know, because she's the saboteur.

684
00:29:39.760 --> 00:29:41.500
She has on a snake belt.

685
00:29:41.780 --> 00:29:44.120
There's a great shot of it right around four minutes.

686
00:29:44.500 --> 00:29:50.800
So it's like this wide black belt, but then the buckle is this slithery gold snake.

687
00:29:51.060 --> 00:29:51.460
Oh.

688
00:29:51.820 --> 00:29:52.540
Snake belt.

689
00:29:52.920 --> 00:29:53.820
I like it.

690
00:29:53.960 --> 00:29:54.600
I looked it up.

691
00:29:54.680 --> 00:29:55.760
There's a gazillion of them.

692
00:29:55.900 --> 00:29:57.260
You can have one, too, if you want.

693
00:29:57.360 --> 00:29:58.920
You can have a snake belt if you want it.

694
00:29:58.920 --> 00:30:01.680
So many versions of it, where the buckle is a snake.

695
00:30:01.900 --> 00:30:02.700
I don't know.

696
00:30:02.840 --> 00:30:04.100
I got a little into it.

697
00:30:04.320 --> 00:30:05.820
Are you going to get a snake belt?

698
00:30:05.920 --> 00:30:06.480
I don't know.

699
00:30:06.820 --> 00:30:07.900
I mean, I might.

700
00:30:08.700 --> 00:30:10.600
What do you wear your snake belt to?

701
00:30:10.740 --> 00:30:11.540
Wherever I want.

702
00:30:11.740 --> 00:30:11.980
Right.

703
00:30:12.060 --> 00:30:12.940
It's your snake belt.

704
00:30:13.100 --> 00:30:13.380
Yeah.

705
00:30:13.640 --> 00:30:15.340
I mean, you know.

706
00:30:15.520 --> 00:30:18.140
Well, Esmeralda's not done being sabotage-y.

707
00:30:18.280 --> 00:30:21.900
She also says that Mare is asexual.

708
00:30:22.240 --> 00:30:22.520
Yeah.

709
00:30:23.000 --> 00:30:27.660
She's going to call out Ned for what she perceives to be his crush on Mare.

710
00:30:27.660 --> 00:30:28.900
Mm, yeah.

711
00:30:29.140 --> 00:30:35.680
All of this, like, his admiration of her, his maybe crush on her, is all fueling just her

712
00:30:36.400 --> 00:30:38.000
need to take him down.

713
00:30:38.920 --> 00:30:42.000
Well, she doesn't want Mare to be the superstar employee.

714
00:30:42.240 --> 00:30:43.800
She's the superstar employee.

715
00:30:44.200 --> 00:30:44.640
Yes.

716
00:30:44.840 --> 00:30:45.040
Yeah.

717
00:30:45.260 --> 00:30:46.220
She's very jealous.

718
00:30:46.600 --> 00:30:50.640
Well, now Ned is going to approach Mare in the copy room, and she says to him,

719
00:30:50.740 --> 00:30:52.780
what are the odds of this paper succeeding?

720
00:30:53.020 --> 00:30:54.620
Well, she's hedging her bets, right?

721
00:30:54.780 --> 00:30:54.960
Mm-hmm.

722
00:30:54.960 --> 00:30:58.560
Do I try to stick with it, or do I leave and go work at this hotel?

723
00:30:58.800 --> 00:31:03.480
He says he thinks their chance of succeeding is 95%.

724
00:31:03.480 --> 00:31:04.260
Yeah.

725
00:31:04.460 --> 00:31:07.800
And then Mare has a talking head where she's like, that's delusional, right?

726
00:31:08.120 --> 00:31:12.300
And a little nerve-wracking because he holds my career in his hands.

727
00:31:12.460 --> 00:31:13.580
But then she wavers.

728
00:31:14.060 --> 00:31:17.540
She's like, but he did sell a crazy amount of toilet paper.

729
00:31:18.100 --> 00:31:20.400
Did you notice something about this talking head?

730
00:31:20.760 --> 00:31:21.760
Clearly you did.

731
00:31:21.760 --> 00:31:22.400
What is it?

732
00:31:22.700 --> 00:31:25.120
It's a two-angle talking head.

733
00:31:25.280 --> 00:31:25.600
Oh.

734
00:31:25.820 --> 00:31:27.160
The camera punches in.

735
00:31:27.260 --> 00:31:32.360
It goes from kind of a medium shot to a close-up midway through the talking head.

736
00:31:32.440 --> 00:31:34.600
And I was like, ooh, it's a new way.

737
00:31:34.800 --> 00:31:35.280
Fancy.

738
00:31:35.280 --> 00:31:43.140
I mentioned earlier that this episode was directed by Yana Gorskaya, who is new to the office directing universe.

739
00:31:43.400 --> 00:31:51.880
And in addition to this talking head having this sort of, like, punch-in, I noticed so many spy shots in this episode.

740
00:31:52.040 --> 00:31:54.260
Like, she really used the spy shot.

741
00:31:54.420 --> 00:31:58.440
I feel like she got the documentary style, and then she built on it.

742
00:31:58.440 --> 00:32:02.320
I just thought, visually, this episode was very interesting.

743
00:32:02.320 --> 00:32:07.160
And it's going to get even more interesting as we use these spy shots later.

744
00:32:07.400 --> 00:32:07.860
At the mattress store.

745
00:32:07.940 --> 00:32:08.260
Exactly.

746
00:32:08.600 --> 00:32:08.780
Yeah.

747
00:32:09.000 --> 00:32:10.120
No, I thought it was really cool.

748
00:32:10.440 --> 00:32:19.000
Well, Ned is so worried that Mare is going to leave the company that he's decided he's going to stick with her for the day and do this mattress story with her.

749
00:32:19.120 --> 00:32:22.340
And Mare says, great, we'll be Woodward and Woodward's boss.

750
00:32:22.340 --> 00:32:24.660
And immediately, Ned says, Ben Bradley.

751
00:32:24.900 --> 00:32:26.840
And Mare's like, oh, of course you knew that.

752
00:32:27.000 --> 00:32:30.700
But then Ned is so giddy, he takes off running.

753
00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:33.860
And Mare says, oh, you run so athletically.

754
00:32:34.320 --> 00:32:36.080
Lady, did this remind you of anything?

755
00:32:36.560 --> 00:32:37.040
No.

756
00:32:37.440 --> 00:32:44.500
When he took off running like that, all I could think about was that scene in Friends when Rachel and Phoebe decide to go for a run.

757
00:32:44.620 --> 00:32:47.620
But Rachel has clearly never seen Phoebe run.

758
00:32:48.080 --> 00:32:53.340
And the way Lisa Kudrow runs as Phoebe is so hilarious.

759
00:32:53.800 --> 00:32:54.940
I do not remember this.

760
00:32:55.000 --> 00:32:55.440
Oh, my God.

761
00:32:55.440 --> 00:32:56.680
It's such a great scene.

762
00:32:56.920 --> 00:32:58.460
I don't know which episode it's from.

763
00:32:58.600 --> 00:33:03.040
But when Ned took off running, all I could think of is, I need to see him run more.

764
00:33:04.720 --> 00:33:07.120
I thought it was really funny physical comedy.

765
00:33:07.600 --> 00:33:10.100
